// M1 — Marketing and generative AI — Session 3. From positioning to messages, without greenwashing
// EU rules on environmental claims: Directive (EU) 2024/825 ("empowering consumers for the green transition"), applicable from 27 September 2026.
$CATEGORY: Master/Marketing/3. Messages and environmental claims

::Specific claim::Which environmental claim is specific and verifiable?{=Upper made from 70 % recycled polyester, certified to the Global Recycled Standard ~Eco-friendly running shoes ~Kind to the planet ~Green by design}

::Neutral by offsetting::Under the EU rules applicable since 27 September 2026, a claim that a product is "climate neutral" because the company offsets its emissions…{=is banned ~is allowed if the offsets are certified ~is allowed if the company is based outside the EU ~is allowed for sports products}

::Hidden trade-off::Highlighting a recycled upper while the sole, the glue and the transport are not addressed is an example of…{=a hidden trade-off ~a point of parity ~a frame of reference ~a reason to believe}

::Generic claims::Under Directive (EU) 2024/825, a generic environmental claim such as "eco-friendly" is…{=banned unless the trader can demonstrate recognised excellent environmental performance ~always allowed in advertising ~allowed if it appears in small print ~allowed if a generative AI wrote it}

::Sustainability labels::In the EU, a sustainability label displayed on a product must be…{=based on a certification scheme or established by public authorities ~designed by the brand's own marketing team ~approved by customer reviews ~printed in green}

::Irrelevant claim::Advertising a product as "CFC-free" when CFCs are banned in such products anyway is an example of…{=an irrelevant claim ~a specific and useful claim ~a point of difference ~a certification label}
